# The loop's EvalContext carries the `evalLoop(...)` call site's surrounding
# source (with the call replaced by the marker), just like the `eval(gen)` form —
# so the loop body can introspect where it is embedded.
>>> {"op":"eval","code":"val src = evalLoop[String] { (ctx, s) => s.complete(ctx.enclosingSource) }"}
~ "ok":true.*__evalBodyPlaceholder__

# The user's case: an evalLoop nested in a `.map` sees the enclosing `.map(...)`.
>>> {"op":"eval","code":"val srcs = List(1, 2, 3).map(x => evalLoop[Boolean]((ctx, s) => s.complete(ctx.enclosingSource.contains(\"map\"))))"}
~ "ok":true.*List\(true, true, true\)

# Call-site locals in scope at the evalLoop call are captured into ctx.bindings;
# here each iteration captures `x`, so every loop sees exactly one binding.
>>> {"op":"eval","code":"val ns = List(1, 2, 3).map(x => evalLoop[Int]((ctx, s) => s.complete(ctx.bindings.length)))"}
~ "ok":true.*List\(1, 1, 1\)
